# client-voice-ack.hex — the peer acknowledging the two full Voice frames of
# the Milestone M1 transmission. Hand-built from RFC 5456 §6.9.1 and §6.10.
#
# > "Upon receiving any media message, except the abbreviated audio and video
# > Mini Frames, an ACK message MUST be sent." (§6.10)
#
# > "An ACK … MUST return the same time-stamp it received. This time-stamp
# > allows the originating peer to determine to which message the ACK is
# > responding." (§6.9.1)
#
# The M1 test transmits one second of tone on a 20 ms grid starting at call
# time-stamp 32,700 ms. Two of those fifty frames go out as full Voice frames:
# the first (the codec pin, §8.1.2) and the one whose time-stamp crosses the
# 0x8000 resync boundary (§6.10). Their time-stamps — and therefore the
# time-stamps echoed here — are 32,700 = 0x7FBC and 32,780 = 0x800C.
#
# Source 0x0042 (the peer), destination 0x0001 (us), type 0x06 IAX,
# subclass 0x04 ACK.
#
# The peer's OSeqno stays at 2 in both: it sent ACCEPT (0) and ANSWER (1), and
# an ACK "does not change the message count" (§7). Its ISeqno advances 2 -> 3
# because each of our full Voice frames is a sequenced full frame (§8.1.1);
# neither the Mini Frames between them nor our own ACKs move any counter.

# ACK of the full Voice frame at 32,700 ms (0x7FBC).
8042 0001 00007fbc 02 02 06 04

# ACK of the full Voice frame at 32,780 ms (0x800C), the 0x8000 resync frame.
8042 0001 0000800c 02 03 06 04
